        "id": "vsf-1-lesson-1",
        "title": "Unit 1 · Tow Types and Vehicle Roles",
        "kind": "reading",
        "body": "## Help the Person and Identify the Tow\n\nSomeone calls and says, “I need my car.” Before you can explain the next step, you need to identify the vehicle and understand why it entered the operation.\n\nKeep these three terms clear:\n\n- **Private property impound (PPI):** A vehicle is towed without the vehicle owner’s consent from private property.\n- **Consent tow:** The owner or an authorized representative authorizes the tow.\n- **Incident management:** Towing and recovery services are coordinated after a roadway incident.\n\nThese terms help you locate the correct process. They do not, by themselves, establish who can collect the vehicle or what charge applies.\n\n## Separate the People Connected to the Vehicle\n\nThe person speaking to you may drive the vehicle every day, but several roles can appear in the records:\n\n- The **registrant** is the person or entity listed in the registration record.\n- The **vehicle owner** holds legal ownership.\n- A **lienholder** has a legal interest connected to a debt, such as vehicle financing.\n\nOne person may fill more than one role, but do not assume all three are the same. Ask for the information needed by the current release procedure and compare it with the record.\n\nUse plain language with the customer. For example: “Let’s confirm the vehicle and the documents that apply to the person collecting it.” That explains the task without turning the conversation into a vocabulary test.\n\n## Connect a Term to an Action\n\nWhen you hear a familiar word, ask what it changes about the task. **PPI** helps identify the tow type. **Registrant** tells you which record is being described. **Lienholder** tells you that another party may have a recorded interest.\n\nThe useful habit is to move from the term to the appropriate record or procedure. Avoid filling a gap with an assumption because the caller sounds confident or the vehicle description is familiar.\n\n## Try a Counter Conversation\n\n:::practice What Would You Do?\n**Hypothetical:** A caller says, “I am the driver, the car is registered to my sister, and the bank still has a loan on it.” Identify the roles you have heard. What do you still need to check before explaining release?\n\nPause and put your response into your own words before opening the coaching.\n:::coach\nThe caller has described a driver, a registrant and a possible lienholder. You have not yet verified the ownership or release authority. Locate the correct vehicle and apply the current document requirements to the person collecting it. You can acknowledge the caller’s situation while still checking the record.\n:::\n\n## Reference Material\n\n:::reference Complete original lesson and source references\n### Tow Types\n\nLearn the terms used before a vehicle release.\n\n- A **private property impound (PPI)** is a tow from private property without the **vehicle owner**’s consent.\n- A **consent tow** is authorized by the owner or an authorized representative.\n- **Incident management** coordinates towing and recovery following a roadway incident.\n\n### Vehicle Roles\n\n- The **registrant** is the person or entity listed in registration records.\n- The **vehicle owner** holds legal ownership.\n- A **lienholder** has a legal interest because of a debt.\n\nThese roles can differ; identify the correct record and applicable release documents rather than assuming the person at the window is all three.\n\n**Source:** Vehicle Storage Facility Glossary Study Guide #1\n:::",
