        "id": "vsf-2-lesson-1",
        "title": "Unit 2 · Vehicle Records and Supporting Documents",
        "kind": "reading",
        "body": "## Match the Document to the Job\n\nA document is useful when you know what it identifies. At a VSF, a vehicle record, a tow truck document and a property parking rule answer different questions.\n\nBefore you rely on a document, ask: **What is this document for, what does it identify, and does it match this situation?** This habit helps you explain a missing or conflicting detail without adding a requirement of your own.\n\n## Read a Cab Card Carefully\n\nA **tow truck cab card** identifies the truck’s **permit type, unit number and VIN**. Compare those details with the truck and the current procedure that applies to the task.\n\nDo not treat an official-looking card as a complete answer to every release question. Use it for the information it actually contains, along with the other documents the current procedure requires.\n\nIf a detail does not match, identify the exact difference. “The VIN on this card differs from the truck information I have” is more useful than “Your paperwork is wrong.” Route unresolved document questions through the appropriate review process.\n\n## Keep Vehicle and Property Records Distinct\n\n**Towbook** holds the vehicle’s operational record, including the condition photographs associated with release. Confirm that the photos and notes belong to the correct vehicle.\n\nA **parking addendum** describes parking terms for a property, such as assigned spaces and other applicable rules. Read the current account instructions when a property rule matters. A rule remembered from another property or an earlier manager may not answer today’s question.\n\nThese records work together: one helps establish what happened to the vehicle, while another helps explain the property’s parking terms.\n\n## Use a Clear Verification Sequence\n\n1. Identify the task and the vehicle or truck involved.\n2. Check the identifiers on the relevant document.\n3. Compare the document with the current record and approved procedure.\n4. Explain any unresolved difference factually.\n5. Record or hand off the review so the next person does not repeat the same guesswork.\n\n## Spot the Mismatch\n\n:::practice What Would You Do?\n**Hypothetical:** A driver presents a cab card with a unit number you recognize, but the VIN does not match the truck information in front of you. A customer is also asking whether their assigned-space rule changed. Which records answer the two questions?\n\nPause and put your response into your own words before opening the coaching.\n:::coach\nUse the cab card and truck record to resolve the identifier mismatch through the current process. Use the property’s current parking addendum and account instructions for the assigned-space question. Familiarity with a unit number or a property does not resolve either difference.\n:::\n\n## Reference Material\n\n:::reference Complete original lesson and source references\n### Tow Truck Documents\n\nA **cab card** identifies the tow truck’s permit type, unit number and vehicle identification number. Review each document against the vehicle and follow the current release procedure.\n\n### Vehicle and Property Records\n\n**Towbook** holds the vehicle record, including the condition photographs taken for release. A **parking addendum** documents parking terms for a property, such as assigned spaces and other applicable rules. Read the property’s current account instructions instead of relying on an old memory of its rules.\n\n**Source:** Vehicle Storage Facility Glossary Study Guide #2\n:::",
