        "id": "vsf-2-lesson-2",
        "title": "Unit 2 · Fees, Courtesy Releases and Evidence",
        "kind": "reading",
        "body": "## Explain Charges and Exceptions Clearly\n\nA customer looking at a total may see one large number. Your job is to explain the verified items that make it up and the process that applies.\n\nKeep **towing charges** and **vehicle-storage charges** distinct. A **winch operation** uses a cable to move a vehicle. Knowing what a service means helps you read the record; it does not authorize you to add a charge from memory.\n\nUse the current approved fee schedule and authorization process before quoting or collecting a live amount.\n\n## Understand a Courtesy Release\n\nA **courtesy release** is an authorized release without charge under company policy. It is an approved exception, so the approval and reason need to remain with the vehicle or account record.\n\nIf a customer asks you to waive charges, acknowledge the request and explain the review path. For example: **“I can help get the request to the person who can review it. I cannot confirm a no-charge release until that approval is recorded.”**\n\nThis is honest and useful. It also keeps the next shift from having to decide whether a conversation was an actual approval.\n\n## Capture the Release Condition\n\nTake the **release-condition photographs within 30 minutes before pickup**, as required by this training’s release workflow. Keep them with the correct vehicle in Towbook.\n\nThe timing matters because the photographs should show the condition near the release, not only when the vehicle first arrived. If pickup is delayed, review the timing requirement again rather than assuming the earlier images still satisfy it.\n\nCheck the vehicle identifier, image timing and storage location before finishing. An image that cannot be found under the right vehicle is difficult for a later reviewer to use.\n\n## Make the Handoff Complete\n\nBefore handing the task to someone else, make clear which charges are verified, whether an exception was requested or approved, and whether release photos are current. Keep factual notes separate from a customer’s allegation or your own assumption about the outcome.\n\nThe customer should understand the next step, and the next employee should be able to continue without rebuilding the conversation.\n\n## Handle Two Open Items\n\n:::practice What Would You Do?\n**Hypothetical:** A customer says someone promised a courtesy release, but you cannot find the approval. The release photos were taken an hour ago. What needs attention before the release task is complete?\n\nPause and put your response into your own words before opening the coaching.\n:::coach\nVerify the claimed approval through the authorized process and record the result. Do not turn the customer’s statement into an approval by repeating it as fact. Address the photo timing so the release record meets the within-30-minutes requirement. Explain both next steps calmly.\n:::\n\n## Reference Material\n\n:::reference Complete original lesson and source references\n### Towing and Storage\n\nA **winch operation** uses a cable to move a vehicle. The source guide distinguishes towing charges from vehicle-storage charges; follow the current approved fee schedule and authorization process before quoting or collecting any charge.\n\n### Courtesy Releases\n\nA **courtesy release** permits a no-charge release when authorized under company policy. It is an approved exception, not a personal promise from the dispatcher. Keep the approval and reason in the vehicle/account record.\n\n### Release Photographs\n\nThe source guide calls for release-condition photographs within **30 minutes before pickup**. Keep the images with the vehicle in **Towbook** so later shifts can find them.\n\n**Source:** Vehicle Storage Facility Glossary Study Guide #2\n:::",
