        "id": "vsf-3-lesson-1",
        "title": "Unit 3 · Professionalism and De-Escalation",
        "kind": "reading",
        "body": "## Make the First Minute Helpful\n\nA caller may be worried, angry or confused before you say hello. You cannot choose their mood, but you can choose a clear opening.\n\n**Dialpad** routes incoming calls through the computer and phone system. Once connected, listen for what the caller needs and verify the correct vehicle record before giving a detailed explanation.\n\nA calm greeting, one clear question and a short explanation of what you are checking can help the person stay with the process.\n\n## Use Three Skills Together\n\n**Professionalism** means keeping a respectful, calm manner. You can speak firmly without insulting or arguing.\n\n**Empathy** means recognizing the person’s frustration and responding thoughtfully. “I understand this has disrupted your day” acknowledges the effect without deciding fault or promising an exception.\n\n**De-escalation** means helping reduce tension and move the interaction toward a useful next step. Slowing your pace, asking one question at a time and explaining what happens next can make the process easier to follow.\n\nThese skills work together. A quiet voice is helpful, but the customer also needs accurate information and a path forward.\n\n## Help the Customer Become Informed\n\nAn **informed customer** understands the necessary steps, forms and documents. Explain them in the order the person needs to act.\n\n1. Confirm what the person is asking for.\n2. Verify the vehicle and relevant record.\n3. Explain the current approved next step in plain language.\n4. Ask whether the person needs clarification.\n5. Record or hand off any unresolved issue.\n\nIf you need to check a detail, say so. “Let me verify that requirement” is more helpful than a fast answer that sends the customer back with the wrong information.\n\n## Try a Calmer Opening\n\n:::practice What Would You Do?\n**Hypothetical:** A caller begins, “You people stole my car, and no one is helping me.” Write a response that acknowledges the concern and starts the correct task.\n\nPause and put your response into your own words before opening the coaching.\n:::coach\nTry: “I can hear that this is upsetting. I can help check the vehicle record and explain the next steps. Let’s start with the vehicle information.” Avoid debating the accusation in the opening sentence. Verify the facts and follow the process as the conversation develops.\n:::\n\n## Reference Material\n\n:::reference Complete original lesson and source references\n### Phone System\n\n**Dialpad** routes incoming calls through the computer/phone system. Before responding, establish what the caller needs and verify the vehicle record.\n\n### Customer Service\n\n- **De-escalation** aims to calm tension and resolve conflict.\n- **Professionalism** means a respectful, calm demeanor.\n- **Empathy** means understanding the customer’s frustration while explaining the process clearly.\n- **Making an informed customer** means explaining the necessary steps and documents, not promising an exception you cannot authorize.\n\n**Source:** Vehicle Storage Facility Study Guide #3\n:::",
