        "body": "## Use Authority in a Way People Can Understand\n\nA dispatcher may know the process while the customer is hearing it for the first time. That difference gives you a responsibility to explain, not a reason to win an argument.\n\nUse **authority** fairly: apply the current approved procedure, describe the available next step and stay within your role. Do not make threats, insult the customer or add a requirement because the conversation is difficult.\n\n## Stay Firm and Show Care\n\nA respectful response can include both a boundary and help: **“I cannot approve that exception, but I can explain the review process and help you identify what is needed.”**\n\nListen for the actual need. A person who repeats the same question may be confused, worried about cost or trying to arrange transportation. **Sensitivity** means taking that context seriously while keeping the explanation accurate.\n\nParking enforcement helps protect access and address parking-related safety and operational problems. Explain the relevant process without turning a disagreement into a personal judgment about the customer.\n\n## Recognize When Safety Changes the Task\n\nAn upset person and a threatening situation call for different responses. If conduct becomes threatening, follow the current safety and escalation policy and protect yourself and coworkers. Do not keep arguing in order to finish an explanation.\n\nRecord the reported or observed behavior factually through the appropriate process. Avoid labels that substitute for what actually happened. A clear account of words, actions, time and location is easier for another person to review.\n\n## Reset Between Difficult Interactions\n\nA hard call can affect the next call. A brief recovery break between interactions, when coverage and the work process allow it, can help you return to a calm tone. A short pause or calming music during an appropriate break is one example used in this training.\n\nNotice when you are speaking faster, becoming short with people or carrying the last customer’s frustration into the next conversation. Ask for the support or coverage you need. Professional service is easier to maintain when you recognize those signs early.\n\n## Practice a Fair Boundary\n\n:::practice What Would You Do?\n**Hypothetical:** A customer asks you to ignore a required step because they have waited a long time. When you explain the process, they raise their voice. What could you say, and what would make you move to the safety process?\n\nPause and put your response into your own words before opening the coaching.\n:::coach\nAcknowledge the wait, explain the specific step and offer the available next action. Do not trade a required check for a quieter conversation. If behavior becomes threatening, use the safety and escalation process instead of continuing a contest over who is right.\n:::\n\n## Reference Material\n\n:::reference Complete original lesson and source references\n### Authority and Safety\n\nUse **authority** fairly and responsibly. Explain the current approved procedure and available next steps without insulting, threatening, or arguing with a customer. Parking enforcement protects access and helps prevent safety risks; do not turn a disagreement into a personal contest.\n\n### Composure and Escalation\n\n**Sensitivity** requires listening and responding respectfully. Brief recovery breaks between difficult interactions can help maintain composure. If conduct becomes threatening, follow the **current safety/escalation policy** and keep yourself and coworkers safe.\n\n**Source:** Vehicle Storage Facility Study Guide #3\n:::",
