        "id": "vsf-3-lesson-3",
        "title": "Unit 3 · Scenario Practice and Evaluation",
        "kind": "reading",
        "body": "## Practice With a Person, Then Review What Happened\n\nA live scenario lets you practice listening, checking the record and explaining a next step while someone responds in real time. Work with a supervisor who can observe the interaction and give specific feedback.\n\nTreat the scenario as practice, not a performance of a memorized script. A useful response changes when a fact changes, a document is missing or the person asks a different question.\n\n## Know the Five Skills Being Reviewed\n\nThe supervisor uses five criteria, each scored **1–5**, for a **25-point total**:\n\n- **Professionalism:** Your tone and behavior stay calm and respectful.\n- **Authority:** You apply the process fairly and explain boundaries clearly.\n- **Empathy:** You recognize the person’s concern while keeping the response accurate.\n- **Knowledge of Rules & Procedures:** You use the correct process and verify uncertain details.\n- **Customer Service:** You help the person understand and take the next available step.\n\nThe training ratings are **21–25 Excellent**, **16–20 Good**, **11–15 Needs Improvement** and **10 or below Unsatisfactory**. The evaluator’s notes explain the observed performance; keep those notes separate from the multiple-choice knowledge-check result.\n\n## Run a Realistic Practice Round\n\nAsk the supervisor to give you a fictional vehicle and customer situation. Practice the greeting, record check, document explanation and closing handoff. Then have the supervisor change one fact, such as a missing approval or a customer who needs the explanation repeated.\n\nThe original exercise includes a **sample $20.27 payment**. Use that only as a training amount. It is not a live vehicle quote or permission to process a real charge.\n\nAfter the round, identify one thing you did well and one action to improve. Ask for feedback tied to words or actions the evaluator actually observed.\n\n## Plan the Next Attempt\n\n:::practice What Would You Do?\n**Hypothetical:** Your evaluator says you stayed polite but did not explain which document the customer needed or what to do next. Which skills need practice, and how would you improve the next round?\n\nPause and put your response into your own words before opening the coaching.\n:::coach\nKeep the calm tone, then make the process specific. Practice checking the current requirement, explaining the needed document in plain words and confirming the customer’s next action. That strengthens procedural knowledge and customer service without changing the rule or promising an exception.\n:::\n\n## Reference Material\n\n:::reference Complete original lesson and source references\n### Scenario Evaluation\n\nPractice customer interactions with a supervisor. The original Unit 3 exam provides a five-part rubric: **Professionalism**, **Authority**, **Empathy**, **Knowledge of Rules & Procedures**, and **Customer Service**. Each criterion is scored 1–5 for a 25-point total.\n\n### Performance Ratings\n\nThe rubric describes **21–25** as Excellent, **16–20** as Good, **11–15** as Needs Improvement, and **10 or below** as Unsatisfactory. Record the evaluator’s notes separately from the multiple-choice knowledge-check result.\n\n### Training Exercise\n\nThe original live scenario instructs the learner to collect a sample **$20.27** payment as an exercise. This is a training amount, not a live vehicle quote.\n\n**Source:** Vehicle Storage Facility Study Guide #3\n:::",
